WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.922
This slide clarifies three related terms: module, package, and library.

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:09.972
A module is one Python file with reusable code.

00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:15.092
It might be a built-in module like math, or it might be a file you write yourself,

00:00:15.092 --> 00:00:20.822
such as calculations.py. A package is a folder that groups related modules.

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:25.487
For example, an analytics folder might contain cleaning.py, metrics.py, and charts.py.

00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:29.007
A library is a larger reusable collection of tools.

00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:33.147
Libraries often include packages and modules.

00:00:33.147 --> 00:00:39.442
NumPy and Pandas are examples of libraries that many data analysts use.

00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:44.742
These words are sometimes used casually, and people may not always use them perfectly.

00:00:44.742 --> 00:00:47.627
But the distinction is still helpful.

00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:53.532
The simple rule is: module equals file, package equals folder,

00:00:53.532 --> 00:00:55.302
and library equals reusable collection of tools.

00:00:55.302 --> 00:01:02.942
Understanding this vocabulary will help when you install and use Python tools later.

00:01:02.942 --> 00:01:10.252
When someone says to import a library, they usually mean to bring in a reusable toolset from a

00:01:10.252 --> 00:01:11.410
package or module.
